"""v83.5 image-checked brand style rewrites (pilot of 5). Each was compared with several product images viewed in the browser pane (Bing image grids)."""
B = [
('Braun', 'Europe', '1950s-1970s', 'tech', 'In the style of Braun, calm functional German industrial design of the 1950s to 1970s. Plain rectangular boxes with softly radiused corners in matte off-white or pale grey metal, or matte black plastic, with natural light beech wood side cheeks and a clear hinged acrylic lid on the audio pieces. Controls are small round push buttons and short slide switches set in tidy grids, with a single tiny accent of orange, yellow, green or red on the key control, and a flat grey liquid-crystal display on calculators. A small lowercase logo sits at a top corner. Speaker grilles are horizontal slots or fine perforations across the lower front. Alarm clocks are square cream or white cases with a plain round white dial, black hands and a thin yellow second hand. Lettering is thin, evenly spaced grotesque sans-serif in black or white. Everything is quiet, honest, ornament-free, precisely aligned and surrounded by empty space.'),
('Vespa', 'Europe', '1946-1960s', 'transport', 'In the style of Vespa, early Italian step-through scooters with a smooth one-piece pressed-steel shell body. A wide curved leg shield sweeps down from a small round headlamp mounted on the handlebar head, and a narrow rounded front mudguard is fixed to the fork and turns with the wheel. A swollen rounded engine cover bulges at the rear on one side, a spare wheel sits on the other, and a flat black saddle rides on a visible coil spring. Small wheels carry slim white-wall tyres. The paint is a soft, slightly dusty enamel in sage green, pale blue-grey, ivory, grey and cream, with narrow chrome trim and little other decoration. A plain horn grille is cast into the headset. Seen in profile it forms a rounded wasp-waisted silhouette. Mood is gentle, practical, optimistic and unmistakably Italian, 1950s holiday charm.'),
('Marimekko', 'Europe', '1960s-present', 'fashion', 'In the style of Marimekko, bold oversized screen-printed patterns on cotton. The best-known print is giant open poppy flowers with five rounded petals and dark centres, cropped by the edge of the cloth, in flat two or three colour combinations such as deep red and pink on white, cherry red on cream, navy and white, blue on pale blue, or cream and peach on rust brown. Other prints are wide vertical stripes, large round dots and simple organic leaf shapes with a uniform flat colour and slightly uneven hand-cut edges. Colour is solid with no shading and no outlines. The prints cover bed linen, curtains, shift dresses with loose boxy shapes, tote bags, cushions, mugs and tableware. The look is fresh, cheerful, graphic and unfussy Nordic modernism, with plain white backgrounds and bright light.'),
('Wedgwood', 'Europe', '18th century-present', 'home', 'In the style of Wedgwood, English jasperware in matte, slightly dry stoneware with a smooth chalky surface. The signature colour is a soft dusty powder blue, with a deeper navy blue in the darker line, and other pieces in sage green, lilac, black and cream. Crisp white relief decoration is applied as thin sprigs and stands out sharply from the plain coloured ground. Slim classical figures in flowing robes, putti, garlands, urns, grapevines, laurel and acanthus leaf borders sit in bands around the body. Shapes are two-handled urns with tall necks, cameo plaques, teapots with a round body and knob finial, cups, bowls and trinket boxes, with rim and base bands of repeated leaves. A mix of blue relief pieces and plain cream glazed tableware often sits together on a mantel or tea table. Neoclassical, symmetrical, delicate and elegant.'),
('Lego', 'Europe', '1970s-present', 'media', 'In the style of Lego, glossy hard plastic bricks in saturated red, blue, yellow, white, black, green and grey, with a rectangular modular body and a grid of round studs on top, crisp moulded edges and sharp seam lines. Small figures have cylindrical yellow heads, dot eyes and simple smiles, C-shaped hands, blocky printed torsos and short pegged legs, shown in groups wearing many costumes. Models are built from rectangular bricks and plates with visible stepped edges and stud rows. The boxes are bright with a large photograph of the finished model against a bold colour field, a red square logo with white lettering outlined in yellow and black in the top left corner, an age badge and a piece count, while adult lines use black or dark themed boxes. The classic storage box is a translucent yellow-lidded bin full of loose colourful bricks. Instruction booklets show clean line diagrams with numbered steps. Lighting is bright, clean toy photography on white or coloured backdrops.'),
]
